From 5e70434dec26e30d9e84ab6b09b8f2631f253924 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 22:04:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] plan9: cgo on amd64 (9front, APE, 9front-gcc)

- cgo on plan9/amd64 (cmd/dist, internal/platform); it always links
  externally: cmd/link writes an ELF object when it links externally
  for Plan 9 (lib.go after determineLinkMode, asmb.go), and the host's
  gcc (9front-gcc's, against APE) makes an ELF program, which the gcc
  port's kernel loads. Without cgo nothing changes (a.out).
- The runtime's TLS pointer is _goprivates, not _privates
  (cmd/internal/obj/x86, rt0_plan9_*.s): in a cgo program APE's start
  owns _privates and slot 0 is the pthread's; runtime/cgo/gcc_plan9.c
  points _goprivates at APE's slots so that g is in slot 1. _goprivates
  and _tos are exported to the C side (runtime/cgo/plan9.go).
- In a cgo program APE's malloc has the break: the runtime's heap is a
  segment of its own (segattach "memory" at heapSegBase, grown with
  segbrk), made in osinit before there is another proc.
- runtime/cgo's POSIX part (pthreads) builds for plan9; cgoBindM is
  allowed there; minit sets procid for runtime/cgo's threads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
